  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b51c85930a5b93bcf7f3e7537cd39cf4134d93470b580c0f7276ddb58ade7297.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=4.$ Substituting $r=4$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
